  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">MFA4: Justice for a Safer Stronger Future, The Hague Institute for Innovation of Law</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The programme &apos; Justice for Safer and Stronger Future&apos; aims at addressing the justice gaps that exists between the demand for justice and the services provided, and focusses in particular on marginalised groups.

The programme intends to improve how people and businesses resolve everyday justice problems and to make justice systems more accessible, fair, and responsive through &apos;people-centred justice approaches&apos;. It will be implemented in Ethiopia, Tunisia, Kenya, Mali, Niger, Burkina Faso, Syria, and Lebanon, and potentially in Iraq. 

The proposal also includes regional and global functions that support the country work through research, advocacy, policy influence, communications, strategic initiatives, and learning. Knowledge research is intended to generate comparative evidence, thematic reports, measurement tools, and learning products across the portfolio, while policy advocacy and communications focus on global narrative-shaping and coalition work. These global functions are meant to reinforce the country programmes rather than sit apart from them.

In HiiL&#x2019;s Theory of Change the goal is to advance people-centred justice so that people can resolve their justice problems more fairly, affordably and quickly, improving safety, dignity, trust and stability. 
The ToC groups the main outcomes into four reinforcing areas: 
- Embedding people-centred approaches and policy in justice systems; 
- Designing and delivering justice services based on analysis and data;
- Enabling supportive regulatory and financing environments for justice systems focussing on the needs of people; 
- Measuring the functioning of justice services by outcomes that matter to users.

The policy relevance of the programme lies mainly in the link between access to justice, stability, and inclusive development. By helping resolve disputes over land, family matters, work, business, and legal identity more fairly and at an earlier stage, the programme seeks to reduce grievances, strengthen trust in institutions, support social cohesion, and contribute to more resilient and legitimate governance in fragile and transition settings. Ultimately this will contribute to stability and security for the target countries itself, but also for the Netherlands. 

The programme also fits well with the Dutch policy because it connects rule of law and access to justice to security, stability, and democracy. In that sense, it supports not only conflict prevention and resilience, but also more accountable and responsive institutions, which are essential for democratic legitimacy and a stronger social contract.</narrative>
  </description>
